Shared GPU resource handles and metadata notifications
=====================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Proposal.md)
Status: Phase 1 complete after second review and independent audit. Runtime migration has not started.

Goal and governing contracts
  Extract shared native identity, validity and allocation-wide existing usage-guard state into
  GpuResourceHandle. Retain wrapper disposal, ownership and deletion scheduling in GpuResource.
  StateCache provides one common lookup and notification mechanism for all supported owning and
  non-owning resource wrappers. Typed wrappers implement abstract HandleMetadataChange.
  Handles identify one native object incarnation by context generation, resource kind and native-width
  ID. They never own storage, delete on release, retarget to replacements or regain validity after deletion.
  Preserve existing context/thread restrictions, ownership, guard rules, scheduling, render order,
  shader algorithms, content readiness and frame qualification. Reuse existing guard enforcement.
  No GpuResourceLifetime, dedicated borrowed-resource registry, duplicate binding/validation cache,
  shared-context namespace generalization, or producer teardown hook duplicating GL notifications.
  Keep identity, common registration/dispatch, typed metadata handling and native adapters in files
  with those respective responsibilities. Follow repository comment and region conventions.
  Use subagents for every build/test invocation. Keep source, unit, GPU integration, package and live
  evidence distinct. Never launch the game for visual acceptance. Creating this plan changes no runtime.

Resolved design decisions and implementation gates
  ☑ Determine resource kinds and represented fields from source, and ALL metadata-altering GL commands
    and overloads from the API/extension specifications, regardless of current usage;
    do not equate existing EngineStateCallMap coverage with interception of all GL operations.
  ☑ Establish how command success and refresh failure are represented without changing existing
    error handling; command arguments alone must not certify a rejected operation's result.
  ☑ Specify retiring-versus-deleted handle state and queued-deletion identity checks, preserving
    existing scheduling and preventing delayed work from affecting a reused native name.
  ☑ Select non-owning wrapper association and expired-entry cleanup mechanics with bounded retention.
  ☑ Resolve unsupported external command paths before enabling persistent tracking for their consumers.
    Conservative handling is required for gaps; an unresolved required path is not completed coverage.
  Decisions are recorded in Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md and its API coverage matrix.
  Command support itself remains implementation work in later phases.
  These are implementation decisions within the proposal. Any required scope change must be reported
  for explicit review rather than silently weakening acceptance or broadening the architecture.

Phase 1: Inventory resource contracts and capture the baseline
  Outcome: the complete wrapper/command coverage and comparison conditions are explicit.
  Inventory and selected mechanics: [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Inventory.md).

  ☑ Read the source proposal and current repository instructions; inventory GpuResource subclasses,
    GpuResourceKind values, constructors/acquisition, context-free declarations, ownership, Detach,
    ReleaseHandle, disposal, manager queues and existing pass/lifetime enforcement points.
  ☑ Produce a coverage matrix mapping each represented metadata field to its changing commands,
    all overloads, bound-target/DSA/multi-object/extension addressing and notification route.
    Derive command completeness from the OpenGL API, not a current-call-site inventory.
    Include texture storage/mips, buffer storage and represented VAO configuration; explicitly identify
    types without mutable metadata and resource kinds currently deleted directly through GL.
  ☑ Inspect EngineStateCalls, EngineStateCallMap and target discovery against the complete API set.
    Use native cloud/depth paths only as integration examples for
    mutation and deletion call sites, including list deletion, framebuffer rebuild and cleanup.
    Record which external acquisition paths can provide continuing command coverage.
  ☑ Record shader validation assumptions about wrapper identity and immutable metadata, existing
    engine-boundary restoration, image bindings and context retirement dependencies.
  ☑ Resolve and document the decision gates above with concrete interfaces and state transitions.
    Separate proposal requirements from chosen implementation mechanics.
  ☑ Capture reproducible baseline source/binary identity, configurations, fixture inputs and tooling.
    Have subagents run relevant existing tests and canonical build/package verification; preserve
    baseline failures. Retain baseline artifacts sufficient for matched comparison after integration.
    Capture actual native-bind and metadata-query counts for cloud/depth paths and corresponding
    output/restored-state checks. Record instrumentation limitations without substituting estimates.
  ☑ Completion: every existing wrapper and applicable API command family/variant has an explicit
    implementation and verification disposition, decisions
    are recorded, and baseline receipts support attributable comparison. No runtime migration yet.

Phase 2: Establish shared handles and the common resource mechanism
  Depends on Phase 1.
  Outcome: common identity and registration primitives are testable before changing consumer behavior.

  ☐ Introduce GpuResourceHandle with immutable native-width identity and context/kind separation,
    validity/retirement state and metadata revision. No native-owning disposal or finalizer.
  ☐ Add common StateCache lookup/acquisition, association removal and context retirement handling.
    Live aliases reuse a handle; deletion removes its entry; reused IDs create fresh handles.
    Initial external acquisition validates resource kind and metadata under the established coverage
    contract, without requiring producer-specific allocation certificates.
  ☐ Implement non-owning wrapper associations, explicit unregister and expired-association cleanup.
    Ensure repeated acquisition cannot create competing live identities after wrapper collection.
  ☐ Define metadata-change descriptions and dispatch support with typed metadata-family information.
    Keep common identity independent of resource-specific binding effects and wrapper metadata queries.
  ☐ Prepare allocation-wide state for existing usage guards without introducing duplicate guard policy.
  ☐ Have subagents verify identity sharing, kind/context separation, native-width IDs, irreversible
    revocation, name reuse, no deletion on handle release, cleanup and context retirement.
  ☐ Completion: primitives satisfy identity/retention contracts; existing wrappers remain operational.
    New tracking is not advertised to consumers before lifecycle and command coverage are integrated.

Phase 3: Integrate wrapper ownership and native retirement
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: existing resource implementations use shared handles with correct ownership and deletion.

  ☐ Refactor GpuResource and its existing implementations to reference shared handles while retaining
    wrapper-local disposal and ownership. Combine wrapper state and handle validity in validity checks.
    Preserve initialization of CPU declarations and existing originating-context restrictions.
  ☐ Move only alias-wide pass/lifetime guard state to the shared handle; reuse current checks and
    release paths. Verify references cannot bypass existing guard rules by using another wrapper.
  ☐ Integrate GpuResourceManager scheduling: mark retiring allocations unavailable to managed use,
    carry originating identity through queued work and reject stale deletion of replacement objects.
    Preserve eventual native deletion and its binding effects in the originating context.
  ☐ Route every in-scope resource kind's deletion through common StateCache notification, including
    current direct-GL deletion branches. Extend exact native adapters and ALL applicable overload/list/extension paths.
    Preserve existing error handling and command ordering; do not assume whole-list rollback.
  ☐ Preserve borrowed disposal as association/local-state retirement only. Preserve Detach as ownership
    transfer with surviving shared identity; borrowed transfer remains rejected. Observed external
    deletion must prevent later owning-wrapper disposal from deleting a reused name.
  ☐ Retain resource-specific texture/image/boundary retirement and actual implicit-unbinding behavior.
    Context retirement must not issue deletion or restore names into another context generation.
  ☐ Have subagents test owning/borrowed aliases, repeated disposal, external deletion, same-name reuse,
    queued deletion, detach, guards, context retirement and unaffected bindings/restoration.
    Exercise native deletion through actual EngineStateCallMap redirection, not only direct fixtures.
  ☐ Completion: all inventoried lifecycle paths pass; no parallel borrowed lifetime mechanism exists.
    Persistent consumer metadata remains gated on the next phase's complete mutation coverage.

Phase 4: Implement metadata notifications and revision-aware validation
  Depends on Phase 3.
  Outcome: observed metadata changes are reflected in every associated wrapper before dependent use.

  ☐ Add abstract HandleMetadataChange to GpuResource and explicit implementations for every concrete
    resource type. Types without relevant mutable metadata explicitly express that disposition.
  ☐ Implement the Phase 1 command matrix through StateCache and existing native call adapters.
    Reconcile VGE direct calls to use the same notification route; cover used bound-target and DSA
    variants, extension equivalents and all managed overloads, whether currently used or not. Resolve bound resources using existing unknown-state recovery.
  ☐ Dispatch after native operations with proven resulting metadata. Use established command values
    where sufficient and targeted queries otherwise. Rejected commands must not publish invented state.
    Failed refresh marks affected metadata unavailable; dispatch must not leave other wrappers falsely
    current if one handler fails. Preserve existing error handling and context/thread contracts.
  ☐ Advance metadata revision for surviving-object metadata changes without replacing handle identity.
    Update existing shader/input validation history and dependent publication checks to revalidate
    changed metadata. Keep complete-input failure behavior and avoid a second validation cache.
  ☐ Ensure metadata notifications do not change content readiness or frame qualification contracts.
  ☐ Have subagents test alias refresh, dimensions/format/mips, buffer size and represented VAO state;
    bound/DSA and unknown-state resolution; rejected operations and refresh failure; context isolation;
    previously validated inputs after incompatible changes; and stable-input binding suppression.
    Exercise required native commands through actual redirection and reconcile every API matrix row. Capability-limited hardware must not silently waive support.
  ☐ Completion: all required metadata paths are covered and stale metadata cannot remain usable.
    Handle continuity, validation revision changes and native binding knowledge remain distinct.

Phase 5: Integrate persistent cloud and depth resources
  Depends on Phases 3 and 4 and their verified native coverage.
  Outcome: cloud/depth consumers use the common resource mechanism without repeated stable acquisition.

  ☐ Update CloudNativeMapAdapter/CloudNativeRuntime and cloud consumers to retain common tracked
    native map wrappers across stable object lifetimes; reacquire after deletion, refresh after metadata
    changes and reject invalid resources before dependent use.
  ☐ Integrate native primary/visibility and liquid depth acquisition with ScreenResourceManager and
    DepthHierarchyRenderer as applicable. Verify native rebuild/replacement paths use covered commands.
    Share existing owned material depth directly where GpuTexture is accepted.
  ☐ Migrate eligible raw sampler inputs to typed wrappers and ordinary StateCache binding behavior.
    Retain conservative paths for unsupported raw IDs; record their exact coverage limitations.
  ☐ Reconcile existing allocation hooks: remove lifecycle-only work made redundant by StateCache;
    retain observations needed by CloudMapPublication and content generation. Preserve world/owner
    selection, uploads, frame readiness, synchronous use and consumer disposal authority.
  ☐ Have subagents verify cloud/depth replacement, same-name reuse, surviving-object metadata changes,
    rebuild failures, world/context teardown, stale content rejection and direct owned-resource sharing.
    Confirm unknown/external binding state still binds correctly and stable known slots skip binds.
  ☐ Completion: intended consumers use common tracking with verified coverage and unchanged content
    qualification. No lifecycle-only producer teardown hook or dedicated borrowed registry is needed.

Phase 6: Qualify the integrated design and reconcile documentation
  Depends on Phase 5.
  Outcome: a reproducible candidate satisfies the proposal with measured costs and explicit evidence limits.

  ☐ Freeze candidate source/binary identity and repeat matched baseline fixtures through subagents.
    Measure actual metadata queries and native binds, separately accounting for registration, dispatch
    and retirement costs. Compare outputs and restored state; disclose regressions and limitations.
  ☐ Have subagents run relevant regression suites and canonical build/package verification for the
    candidate. Verify package identity and required generated assets against tested outputs.
  ☐ Verify bounded associations through repeated acquisition, disposal, collection and context cycles;
    ensure no old wrapper/queued retirement can affect a replacement allocation.
  ☐ Remove temporary migration scaffolding and obsolete duplicated identity/notification paths.
    Reconcile the full command matrix and all temporary compatibility dispositions; retain intentional
    conservative raw-ID support with its documented boundary.
  ☐ After the final matched measurements, remove ResourceCallObserver.cs, its temporary interception,
    VGE_RESOURCE_BASELINE activation and measurement-only fixture methods/calls and output writers.
    Preserve useful correctness assertions as ordinary tests without the observer. Retain measurement
    receipts and the instrumented source/patch needed to reproduce them in baseline artifacts.
  ☐ Have subagents rerun affected correctness tests and canonical build/package verification after
    observer cleanup. Record the final source/binary identity and verify that cleanup changed no runtime
    implementation; distinguish the archived instrumented measurements from the final clean test suite.
  ☐ Update Rendering.ShaderBindingState.md and relevant resource/cloud architecture documentation to
    describe current implemented responsibilities, command coverage, ownership and metadata behavior.
    Keep permanent docs free of phase narratives and removed implementation history.
  ☐ Perform a second review against every proposal section and resolve discrepancies. Record source,
    unit, GPU integration, package and measurement evidence separately; do not claim appearance or
    frame-time benefits from headless checks. Any live appearance acceptance is user-run and separately
    reported; unmeasured runtime performance remains unclaimed.
  ☐ Completion: proposal coverage, regression/package receipts, measurements, cleanup and current-state
    documentation agree for the same candidate; unresolved required work is explicitly left open.

Proposal traceability
  Intent/responsibilities: governing contracts; Phases 2–5.
  Shared identity and context/retention: Phases 1–3 and 6.
  Ownership, disposal, detach, guards and scheduling: Phases 1 and 3.
  Abstract metadata notifications, failures and revisions: Phases 1, 2 and 4.
  StateCache/native coverage and binding behavior: Phases 1, 3 and 4.
  Cloud/depth consumers and content contracts: Phase 5.
  Scope, source layout and documentation: governing contracts; Phases 2–6.
  Acceptance and measurements: baseline in Phase 1; focused verification in Phases 2–5; Phase 6.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Every proposal requirement is implemented and verified, or an explicit deferral is approved with
    its rationale and consequences. Conservative handling alone does not complete a required migration.
  ☐ Shared handles preserve identity, ownership, guard, deletion, transfer and context contracts across
    aliases; released handles never delete native storage and stale work never affects reused names.
  ☐ Metadata notifications and revision-aware validation cover the inventoried supported paths;
    native integration evidence uses actual redirection and unknown/error paths remain correct.
  ☐ Common tracking supports the intended cloud/depth consumers without duplicated lifetime systems,
    while render/content behavior and existing state restoration remain correct.
  ☐ Required tests, package checks, cleanup, documentation and measured call/cost comparisons are
    complete for the qualified candidate. Live appearance and frame-time claims match actual evidence.
